micro_lesson.content:
A frequent pattern is deduplication: converting a list to a set removes duplicate values. Another pattern is finding common elements between two collections using intersection. A third pattern is computing the difference between two sets. Here are examples:

```python
# Deduplication
raw_list = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]
unique = set(raw_list)
print(unique)  # Output: {1, 2, 3}

# Intersection
set_x = {1, 2, 3}
set_y = {2, 3, 4}
common = set_x & set_y
print(common)  # Output: {2, 3}

# Difference
only_in_x = set_x - set_y
print(only_in_x)  # Output: {1}
```

These patterns leverage the O(1) average-time lookup property of sets.

